WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.440
Tim Ferriss: We've talked about a lot the
last few times that we've spoken, but I'd

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:24.110
love to chat maybe about the heart surgery
and your recovery from the heart surgery,

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:31.730
which I think might be perhaps an inspiring
place to start for a lot of people.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:38.500
Would you mind just describing the heart surgery
and what the recovery has looked like for

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.500
yourself?

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.140
Arnold Schwarzenegger: I think that you’re
referring to the most recent one, which was

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.160
2018 or '19.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.179
It was '18.

00:00:50.179 --> 00:01:00.200
That was when I just went into a routine non-invasive
aortic valve replacement.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:07.880
But it goes through your arteries on your
arm, neck, and then your growings into your

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:13.479
heart, and then they replace your valve.

00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:18.659
It's a standard procedure that they have now
in the last 10 years, and you don't have to

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:22.460
perform open heart surgeries anymore because
of it.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:29.810
Just happens to be that in my case, they had
a difficult time somehow and they poked through

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:34.860
the heart wall with the cable.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:43.890
So I got internal bleeding, and they now had
to perform an emergency open heart surgery.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:49.640
And I, of course, was not aware of any of
that, because I was out.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:02:00.560
The next thing, I wake up and I'm really happy
and this is over, only to find out that I

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.409
was having a breathing tube in my throat and
I couldn't talk.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:11.270
And I saw three doctors in front of me not
smiling, but kind of having a concerned look

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:16.610
on their face.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.060
One said, "Don't try to talk, because you
can't.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:24.950
You still have a breathing tube in your mouth,
and we're going to pull that out right away

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.880
now, so just stay with us.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:32.200
Okay, one, two, three."

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:40.129
And then I was — so I was breathing heavy
and someone ripped the breathing tube out

00:02:40.129 --> 00:02:41.410
of my mouth.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:50.540
And then the second doctor said, "We are so
sorry, Arnold, but something went wrong with

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:57.080
the valve replacement and we had to perform
open heart surgery."

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:05.659
So I digested all that and also the breathing
tube was just ripped out of my kind of throat

00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:06.830
and lungs.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.030
So I'm still kind of not saying anything,
just look, staring at them.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:18.810
And the next thing the next doctor says, "Yeah,
it's like 16 hours later now since you were

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:19.920
first put down.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.739
But so now we are keeping you awake and everything
hopefully will be fine.

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:29.110
The most important thing is now for you to
make it through the first night, because that's

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:35.310
usually when you can have pneumonia and where
things go south."

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:42.939
So I was saying to myself, "I've just gotten
out of an open heart surgery where it could

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:49.129
have cost my life, and now they're telling
me that this next night or two is very crucial

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:50.390
so I don't lose my life."

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.689
So I said, "What the Hell is that?

00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:56.650
What kind of a deal did I get into here?"

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:02.830
So anyway, the bottom line is is that it was
— I had to kind of connect quickly, shift

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.440
gears, and realize what has happened.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.970
Which takes you a while because you're on
drugs and you're on medication and you're

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:12.920
still on the anesthesia somewhat and you're
not with the program.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:18.070
So as I slowly started getting with the program,
I had to kind of shift gears and realized

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:21.040
that the simplest things weren't possible
to do.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.340
Couldn't go to the bathroom, I couldn't get
up, I couldn't do this, do that.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:29.620
And then slowly I started getting with the
program, started pulling out the tubes during

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:33.430
the night, and they started adjusting this
and adjusting that.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:39.470
And then eventually, I was able to go and
get up a little bit.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.030
So now the doctor said, "The key thing is
to walk."

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:47.460
Because if you walk, then you exercise your
lungs.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:53.760
And when you exercise your lungs, the danger
of having pneumonia starts really slipping

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.479
away and you don't have to worry about that.

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:01.610
But the key thing that kills you always is
the least amount of lung activities that you

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:07.380
have can create this problem, and you die
with pneumonia.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:11.340
So I was always setting goals for myself,
"Okay, I'm going to go and walk around the

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:12.370
bed.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:16.430
Right away I'm going to get up, have someone
pull along the machines."

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.900
Then after I walked around the bed, I sit
down again, relax a little bit.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.680
Then I went outside to do this thing.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.880
So I've got to make it outside the room, and
I started going outside the room and back

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:30.259
in again, and outside the room, back in again,
and started doing exercises like that.

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:34.460
Then eventually, I was walking around the
nurses' station.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:42.050
And then eventually, two days later, I was
walking down the long hallways over to another

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:45.900
building and back, which was like hundreds
of yards.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:53.559
So I could really build up strength and get
out of that hospital as quick as possible.

00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:58.410
So after six days, rather than what they thought
seven days, after six days, I got out of the

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:02.430
hospital and I was exercising and I was walking.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:09.940
And I asked friends of mine that were working
out with me to put the pressure on me, and

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:14.901
my family, my kids, and everyone, put the
pressure on me to make me walk and do not

00:06:14.901 --> 00:06:16.810
let me get away with not walking.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.710
So that's exactly what we did.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:23.650
Tim Ferriss: You've always seemingly been
very good at setting goals, having a vision,

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.880
and then setting these intermediate goals.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:31.330
I'd like to rewind the clock because I was
trying to find some aspects of your life that

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:32.960
we haven't explored already.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.500
This is going to go back to age 10.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:40.889
So age 10, roughly, is it true that you were
selling ice cream at the time?

00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:44.639
I think they were ice cream pops or some type
of ice cream.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:48.250
You can't believe everything you read on the
internet, but I did find that, and I'm wondering

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:52.940
if that was one of your first experiences
with entrepreneurship, or at least trying

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:54.979
to make money by selling something.

00:06:54.979 --> 00:06:56.960
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Yeah, and you're absolutely
correct.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.509
And it was not that I wanted to become an
entrepreneur or anything like this at that

00:07:02.509 --> 00:07:03.509
point.

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:08.370
What it was was just really a necessity.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:12.990
I felt like I needed a training suit.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:20.150
Friends of mine had training suits in the
winter and tennis shoes, and I didn't, and

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:21.610
my parents refused to buy it.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:27.800
They just would give me my lederhosen, the
pants that they wore day and night, and in

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:29.740
the winter and in the summer.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:37.900
And then some high shoes, boots, and just
closed it with work all the time, but nothing

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:38.900
fancy.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:42.460
So I wanted to have — and I'd go to the
soccer field, I wanted to have a training

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:43.460
suit.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.220
So they said, "Well, you go out and make your
own money, that's fine.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:48.099
You can buy your own stuff.

00:07:48.099 --> 00:07:52.169
But you definitely — you're not going to
get it fast, because that's not the kind of

00:07:52.169 --> 00:07:56.319
money that you have," and that's exactly what
I did.

00:07:56.319 --> 00:08:04.020
So I went downstairs to the lake, where I
grew up and where I learned how to swim, and

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:12.521
I asked — there was a ice cream and dessert
kiosk in front of the big restaurant and right

00:08:12.521 --> 00:08:13.521
near the lake.

00:08:13.521 --> 00:08:22.370
So I asked him, I said, "Do you have anything
where I can go and put ice in it and then

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:27.220
carry it back there where the people are lying
around in the middle of the grass and the

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:33.399
bushes and all around the lake, that they're
too lazy, maybe, to come to the front here

00:08:33.399 --> 00:08:39.169
and buy the ice cream here and have it melt
on the way back there and it's gone already?"

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:41.349
So I said, "There is some people, I think..."

00:08:41.349 --> 00:08:47.860
This was maybe the entrepreneurial kind of
mentality, because I felt kind of there is

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:55.080
maybe a need for someone to deliver the ice
cream to those bushes and those different

00:08:55.080 --> 00:09:00.880
locations around the lake, rather than have
those people go all the way to the front to

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:03.040
get the ice cream.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.930
So I did not know, but I thought that maybe
it would be interesting idea, let's try it.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:14.630
So I would just take a little box that the
guy gave me with some kind of a container

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:21.170
where you normally put water in it, some round
kind of container and with a handle on it.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:30.400
And he put in ice from the winter that when
they cut the ice in the winter on that lake.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:36.610
They used it in the restaurant below for keeping
— because there was no refrigeration yet.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:40.579
Keeping the drinks, the beer, and the vegetables
and everything cold.

00:09:40.579 --> 00:09:45.921
So they had huge amounts of broken ice in
the bottom of that restaurant.

00:09:45.921 --> 00:09:50.310
So the guy had in his trunk, where the ice
cream was, this ice in it.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:58.950
So he gave me a little bit for my container,
and then he put in 20 ice creams.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:02.060
They were just icicles, those bars.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:03.089
He put those in there.

00:10:03.089 --> 00:10:06.720
They had a little bit of cellophane, this
little kind of paper with.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:13.769
So I ran with those around the lake, and I
said, "Ice cream, ice cream, fresh ice cream,

00:10:13.769 --> 00:10:14.769
ice cream."

00:10:14.769 --> 00:10:19.760
And then every so often, someone would pop
up and say, "Yeah, I want some ice cream,"

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.079
and then I would go over there to the bush
and there would be three guys lying there

00:10:23.079 --> 00:10:24.079
with a girl.

00:10:24.079 --> 00:10:26.760
So they said, "Give me four ice creams," I'll
give four.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:27.940
So the next, there were four.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:34.790
So by the time I was a hundred yards gone,
I already was out of ice cream from my pocket.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:39.930
So I had to run back to the front again, get
more ice cream and go back out again.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:45.010
And then eventually, I just took a hundred
with me, and there was enough ice underneath

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:47.930
so to keep it cold in that hot day.

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:50.510
It was around 30, 35 degrees.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:53.029
So I sold this ice cream.

00:10:53.029 --> 00:10:59.470
And then the end of the day, I ended up what
this guy gave me, one shilling for each ice

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:00.470
cream.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:04.470
So I sold like a hundred and forty five, a
hundred fifty, a hundred eighty ice creams,

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.180
and so made 180 shillings.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:10.850
So that got me enough money to buy myself
a training suit.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:16.209
And then the next weekend, I would go back
and I would buy myself with the money then

00:11:16.209 --> 00:11:18.280
some tennis shoes and stuff like that.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:25.150
So this is kind of how I started to realize
that if you work your ass off, you can really

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:26.410
accomplish a lot of things.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:32.649
That's why my book, Be Useful, I put in there
— the main chapter is just "Work Your Ass

00:11:32.649 --> 00:11:33.649
Off."

00:11:33.649 --> 00:11:34.649
Tim Ferriss: Work your ass off.

00:11:34.649 --> 00:11:40.810
And we're definitely directly going to segue
to Be Useful: Seven Tools for Life.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:49.440
This title, Be Useful, could you explain — and
that probably ties into similar chapters around

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:53.890
the story around earning money and working
your ass off in your younger years.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:56.740
Where did this title come from, Be Useful?

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:00.650
Arnold Schwarzenegger: It comes from my father.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:03.470
He would always say that.

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:10.570
And his whole attitude always was, whatever
you do, try to serve the people.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:14.910
Try to do something good for your community
or for your family.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.970
Don't just think about yourself.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:22.550
That's why my father was so heavily kind of
against body building, because he felt like

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:32.290
that there — it was called Selbstfrönung, which
means you are kind of glorifying and you're

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:36.810
treating yourself rather than worrying about
others.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:40.520
So he just thought — like he says, "Instead
of lifting for yourself, why don't you go

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.589
out and chop some wood?

00:12:42.589 --> 00:12:45.279
Why don't you go and shovel some coal?

00:12:45.279 --> 00:12:48.910
This way, you help some older person that
has coal delivered.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:54.390
Shovel coal into their basement so they have
coal in the winter and they have wood in the

00:12:54.390 --> 00:13:00.450
winter, and you help an older person that
is not able to do those things anymore.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:01.450
That's what you should do.

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:03.389
Then you get also muscles.

00:13:03.389 --> 00:13:04.610
Then you also get strong.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:07.149
Then you also can kind of look good.

00:13:07.149 --> 00:13:09.420
Look at those guys like László Papp.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.670
László Papp is a boxer from Hungary.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:14.990
He was the European champion in boxing.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.160
How does he train?

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.430
There's pictures all over the place where
he's chopping wood in the forest.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.269
I said, "That's how he trained.

00:13:22.269 --> 00:13:24.390
That's how he becomes a world boxing champion.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:28.270
He doesn't just think about his boxing, he
thinks about other people too."

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:29.290
So that was his rap.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:31.949
So he says, "You've got to be useful.

00:13:31.949 --> 00:13:36.639
You've got to go and use your talent to help
people."

00:13:36.639 --> 00:13:38.380
So that's where it kind of came from.

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:43.470
And it's something that was really interesting
because I think you and I, we talked about

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:50.709
that in the past, that sometimes things come
to you as a kid, but then later on in life,

00:13:50.709 --> 00:13:52.670
it kind of comes back.

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.850
It's like kind of you're not dead.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:59.040
Six o'clock in the morning and you want to
stay in bed and you say, "Wait a minute,"

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:03.810
I heard this voice from my father screaming,
"Be useful."

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:06.190
People have never accomplished anything.

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:10.490
No country ever was built by people sleeping
in.

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:14.250
Austria was not built by people sleeping in.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.910
America was not built by people sleeping in.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:22.329
People struggled, people suffered, people
worked their asses off to build this country.

00:14:22.329 --> 00:14:25.690
So you want to go now and sleep in.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:31.060
So then you start feeling guilty and you just
jump out of bed right away, because you hear

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:32.060
those sounds.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:39.380
They come back and it's kind of motivational,
because it really has driven me my whole life

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:40.759
and has pushed me.

00:14:40.759 --> 00:14:45.329
So that's why I call the book Be Useful, because
it's kind of a nobler title.

00:14:45.329 --> 00:14:52.230
And then within that book, I put all the chapters
in there, [inaudible], have a clear vision,

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:57.791
or never think small, or work your ass off,
or sell, sell, sell, and all of those kind

00:14:57.791 --> 00:14:58.800
of things.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:14:59.800
Shift gears.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:05.870
Or whatever it is, I've put this kind of lessons
together which were very crucial lessons that

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:10.720
I've learned throughout my life and throughout
the various different careers, but especially

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:11.980
in the gym.

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:16.060
Most of my lessons are learned in the gym,
because there's no better place than to learn

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:21.680
in the gym, because this is where the rubber
hits the road, right?

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:27.360
I mean, this is where if you don't do the
forced reps and if you don't work until it

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:34.430
burns and until it hurts and then you go beyond
that and do the forced reps, you're not going

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:35.430
to grow.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:41.350
So now you get this message that only through
pain you can actually grow.

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:42.550
Only through pain you can grow.

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:48.240
And discomfort and misery, you can kind of
grow also as a person.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.800
Not just physically, not just muscle-wise,
but as a person.

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:54.310
Through comfort, no one ever grows.

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:59.500
You only can grow through comfort and to going
through things where you have to have discipline

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:01.070
and where you struggle.

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:07.589
If it's in the military, if it is in a real
good job or if it's studying in the university,

00:16:07.589 --> 00:16:13.259
the more this kid struggle and study all night
and goes through hardship, the further they're

00:16:13.259 --> 00:16:14.259
going to go.

00:16:14.259 --> 00:16:20.170
Look at the students that go through medical
college and all this stuff, how many sleepless

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:21.680
nights they have and all this.

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:22.680
So this is what it takes.

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:26.370
Tim Ferriss: How did your father say, "Be
useful," in German?

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:27.680
What is the way to say that properly?

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:29.329
Or how would he say to you?

00:16:29.329 --> 00:16:32.269
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Well, he would have
different versions of it.

00:16:32.269 --> 00:16:34.069
He would just say, "[German]."

00:16:34.069 --> 00:16:35.069
Work hard.

00:16:35.069 --> 00:16:36.230
Tim Ferriss: Work hard.

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:37.310
Arnold Schwarzenegger: [German].

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:38.389
Tim Ferriss: [German].

00:16:38.389 --> 00:16:39.470
Arnold Schwarzenegger: [German].

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:41.910
It was always help others.

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.270
Don't just be interested in yourself.

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:48.350
So it was kind of like a combination of all
of those things that he threw together, and

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:54.080
he would just always kind of be very critical
of people that didn't do that.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:57.870
My father's job was a police officer.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:01.589
He was with the gendarmerie, which was the
country police.

00:17:01.589 --> 00:17:06.730
It was all about protecting people and keeping
law and order.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.770
So that's serving the people.

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:13.500
And the same is when you talk about music.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:15.490
Music is to entertain people.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:22.809
So his whole thing about learning to play
music and to play six instruments, the trumpet,

00:17:22.809 --> 00:17:26.049
the [inaudible], the saxophone, the clarinet.

00:17:26.049 --> 00:17:31.880
All of those different instruments made him
a great performer.

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:35.660
He wrote music, he conducted music.

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:39.500
So it was all about what can you do for other
people.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:44.440
So he would go to the city park out there,
and he would have concerts.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:47.039
He would play concerts, he would play in funerals.

00:17:47.039 --> 00:17:50.920
Whenever a police officer died, he would always
play at the funerals.

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:55.169
And direct the music and conduct the music
and all that stuff.

00:17:55.169 --> 00:17:59.090
So he was always interested in serving the
people.

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:01.430
So he was really into that.

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:06.179
Tim Ferriss: Arnold, when I think about you,
the adjective that comes to mind — I was

00:18:06.179 --> 00:18:08.030
asking myself this question earlier today.

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:12.850
The adjective that comes to mind for me is
resilient.

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:16.559
Many people have seen the Netflix miniseries,
Arnold.

00:18:16.559 --> 00:18:19.840
One of the lines, and I'm not going to get
this perfectly right, that stuck out to me

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:24.700
was that your upbringing made you but broke
your brother.

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:28.059
And I'm probably getting the phrasing off
a little bit, but I'm wondering if you could

00:18:28.059 --> 00:18:33.690
just elaborate on that a little bit and speak
to what the upbringing was like, and then

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:38.140
also what made you different from your brother
in that respect.

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:49.030
Arnold Schwarzenegger: I think that my brother
was more in nature, more fragile.

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:57.220
I never really realized that when I kind of
grew up, but just the very fact certain things

00:18:57.220 --> 00:19:02.440
that kind of unfolded made me then realize
that.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:06.080
There were two things.

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:13.100
One of them was that he was more fragile,
and the other one was that he appeared to

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:18.290
be more fragile, and that I appeared to be
strong.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:22.440
The reason I'm saying that is because, for
instance, when he was 11 years old and he

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:29.549
was going to school in Graz, outside the village,
and he had to go with the bus there, then

00:19:29.549 --> 00:19:34.539
they had to be picked up at the bus station
a half an hour away from our house.

00:19:34.539 --> 00:19:40.610
And it was night in the winter, in the fall,
and then he was afraid to go home.

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:43.760
He would say, "I'm afraid to go home by myself."

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:48.309
So my father would turn to me, he says, "Well,
Arnold, can you pick him up?

00:19:48.309 --> 00:19:52.490
I'll give you a shilling every night that
you pick him up."

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:55.330
So I'm going to end of the week, five shilling.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:59.552
Because on Saturday, it was only half day
school, so he would go home at the time when

00:19:59.552 --> 00:20:01.540
he was still alive.

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:05.429
So I said to him, "Yeah, yeah, I'll pick him
up.

00:20:05.429 --> 00:20:06.429
No problem."

00:20:06.429 --> 00:20:07.710
He says, "You're not afraid?"

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:09.340
I said, "You're kidding me.

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:10.340
No."

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:14.170
But in the meantime, I was also scared shitless.

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:20.240
So I appeared tougher than my brother, but
I was also afraid.

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:24.870
But I was not afraid enough not to go.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:27.669
So I did go, even though I was afraid.

00:20:27.669 --> 00:20:31.370
My brother refused to go because he was afraid.

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:36.231
So there was both, that I was a little tougher
than him, but that I also pretended to be

00:20:36.231 --> 00:20:38.010
tougher than him.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:41.520
So that kind of unfolded as time went on.

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:48.380
So as we were punished and beaten and all
of those kind of things that was going on,

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:54.909
it was clear that my brother couldn't quite
handle the thing, because he ran away more

00:20:54.909 --> 00:20:56.440
often from home.

00:20:56.440 --> 00:21:00.230
Well, not only more often, ran away, because
I never ran away.

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:05.490
So he ran away and he would not appear sometimes
for a week.

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:08.310
My father would have to look for him all over
the place, and he was scared.

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:09.640
Did he get lost?

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:11.059
Is he gone?

00:21:11.059 --> 00:21:12.310
Or what is going on?

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:19.250
It freaked him out and we treated him for
a while when he came back home, we treated

00:21:19.250 --> 00:21:26.280
him for a while nicer and then started getting
to be again too much for him.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:34.890
What happened was really when I look back
was that each time a father punished us, it

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:44.480
made my brother more and more vulnerable and
weaker and it made me strong.

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:51.570
I thrived on that kind of like — my mind
started gearing up to, "I'm going to get back

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:52.850
at him.

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:56.440
I'm going to leave this house as soon as I
can.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:58.929
I'm going to be out of here at the age of
18.

00:21:58.929 --> 00:22:03.500
I'm going to go to the military and then I'm
going to go and get my passport.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:07.419
And then I'm going to go to Germany and then
I'm going to go to America and I'm going to

00:22:07.419 --> 00:22:08.419
be out of here.

00:22:08.419 --> 00:22:09.419
This is it.

00:22:09.419 --> 00:22:10.419
I'm not going to take this any longer.

00:22:10.419 --> 00:22:16.750
And it will make me stronger and really set
a program and set a goal and a vision of what

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:18.390
I'm going to do in life."

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:20.070
Whereas my brother crumbled.

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:22.050
He got weaker.

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:24.040
He started drinking.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:28.010
He started getting involved in alcohol.

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:33.160
And I could see in his behavior that he didn't
behave well.

00:22:33.160 --> 00:22:38.130
He was abusive.

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:48.130
And eventually, he died because of a car accident,
drunk driving with the age of — he was I

00:22:48.130 --> 00:22:51.779
think 24 and I was 23 when that happened.

00:22:51.779 --> 00:22:53.840
I was already in America at that time.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:56.100
But it was really sad.

00:22:56.100 --> 00:23:01.640
I could see that he just could not handle
anymore the punishment.

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:02.929
And I could.

00:23:02.929 --> 00:23:04.679
I was thriving on it.

00:23:04.679 --> 00:23:09.520
And I used it to my big plus and as a support
system.

00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:15.779
And it was all about gave me the motivation,
it created the fire in the belly.

00:23:15.779 --> 00:23:19.400
It made me a creative vision, a necessary
vision.

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:20.841
This is what I want to do.

00:23:20.841 --> 00:23:21.890
I want to get to America.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:24.330
I have to become a bodybuilding champion.

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:26.440
I have to get away from home.

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:29.900
I had to find a new father figure.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:34.470
My father was great to be the father, the
official father, but there were others.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:38.419
The trainer and the weightlifting club, [inaudible
00:29:31].

00:23:38.419 --> 00:23:45.600
There was a guy that could now that we also
knew there was in his 40s and 50s that became

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:51.220
a father figure, very smart guy that spoke
English and was very worldly.

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:57.270
And then there was a Jewish fellow there that
became our kind of mentor and helped us with

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:58.780
the weightlifting club.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:02.640
This all became my new father figures in the
way.

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:06.960
And then eventually Joe Weider when I came
to America and all of these people I looked

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:13.299
up right away as an idol because they would
treat me in a better way.

00:24:13.299 --> 00:24:21.270
And they would educate me and they would really
usher me along and nurture me along.

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:24.020
But I never really rescinded my father because
of it.

00:24:24.020 --> 00:24:31.890
I always felt that he served a really extraordinary
purpose for me, not for my brother, but for

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:38.190
me, which means to get me to America, to become
a great champion, to have that will to be

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:46.220
able to work no matter how many hours it takes
to do no matter what it takes.

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:53.490
And do not shy away from misery or from pain
or from obstacles or from falling down and

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:57.690
having to get up again and crawl on all four
from nothing.

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:02.060
That was the power and the strength my father
gave me.

00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:06.730
And so I've always appreciated that.

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:12.050
And nothing comes in the perfect package because
I knew that if he would've given me all the

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:18.149
love and if he would've not done none of that,
and if I would've had all the money in the

00:25:18.149 --> 00:25:20.840
world, I would've not grown up as tough.

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:25.779
And I would not have been able to accomplish
what I did coming to America and becoming

00:25:25.779 --> 00:25:30.320
this world bodybuilding champion and do all
the things that I was doing.

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:34.020
It was all because of that upbringing.

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:41.220
And so when I look at, for instance, my in-laws,
when I see those kids, they're very smart

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:49.360
kids in the Kennedy family, but I always felt
like they couldn't have grown up like Maria

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:53.460
or Maria's brothers or anyone around them.

00:25:53.460 --> 00:25:55.279
They couldn't have grown up.

00:25:55.279 --> 00:26:01.290
Or my children couldn't grow up with the same
desire and the same hunger, but they can get

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:03.120
other qualities.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:05.620
And so that's the key thing to focus then
on that.

00:26:05.620 --> 00:26:13.090
But I mean, they could never have that quality
of hunger and desire and deep inside kind

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:16.590
of reach, being able to reach inside no matter
what it takes.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:20.640
Tim Ferriss: Let's talk about one of the rules:
Never think small.

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:25.870
You seem like the walking archetype of not
thinking small.

00:26:25.870 --> 00:26:30.100
You've lived multiple lifetimes compared to
most people.

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:36.169
How would you suggest people think of never
think small or what stories come to mind that

00:26:36.169 --> 00:26:38.510
from your life exemplify that?

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:41.840
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Well, just the very
beginning.

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:48.290
I mean for me to go and say, "I want to compete
in the Junior Mr. Europe competition," rather

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:51.770
than just in the Mr. Austria competition.

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:54.580
I trained just as hard as everyone else in
the gym.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:56.100
Their goal was just smaller.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:59.000
They said, "I want to be Mr. Austria.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:02.960
And I said, "I want to be Mr. Europe, so I'm
going to start with Mr. Junior, Junior Mr.

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:04.929
Europe, the best built man of Europe.

00:27:04.929 --> 00:27:06.860
I'm going to go to this competition."

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:10.630
And I was thinking bigger and I was training
as hard as they were.

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:11.690
Everything was the same.

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:15.640
But then when I won that competition because
I had a very clear vision, that's what I want

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:23.659
to win, that immediately launched me into
getting a job to become a trainer in Munich

00:27:23.659 --> 00:27:26.600
in a bodybuilding gymnasium.

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:30.080
Now imagine how in Heaven is that.

00:27:30.080 --> 00:27:31.840
You're a young bodybuilder.

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:35.890
You're 18 years old, you just won your first
international competition.

00:27:35.890 --> 00:27:40.850
You win some local competitions in Austria,
you win some power lifting competitions, some

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:47.850
weight lifting competitions, but now you are
Junior Mr. Europe, and you have this trophy,

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:53.409
and now you're getting a job to train in the
second-biggest gym in Munich.

00:27:53.409 --> 00:27:55.010
That was like absolute Heaven.

00:27:55.010 --> 00:28:00.690
With 19, I started training, become the trainer
in the gym.

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:05.750
Now I had the opportunity to train day and
night.

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:09.390
When I wake up, because I was sleeping in
the gym.

00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:12.230
I was waking up and I was training.

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:13.610
I was taking a nap in the afternoon.

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:15.090
I was training.

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:19.390
I was going to sleep before going to sleep
at night, after dinner, I was training.

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:21.330
I was training day and night.

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:22.419
This is a dream.

00:28:22.419 --> 00:28:25.260
But it was all because I thought big.

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:31.980
They were still stuck working for some bathhouse
in the Austria or for the government or being

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:35.410
a trash collector or being a teacher or something
like that.

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:37.289
They were still stuck in the same job.

00:28:37.289 --> 00:28:43.360
I was already moving on to Munich and I was
already a trainer in the bodybuilding gymnasium,

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:48.690
making this the launchpad to America, which
was my ultimate dream.

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:49.690
This is what I'm saying.

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:53.610
It didn't take more work to think big.

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:55.050
It just thinking big.

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:57.700
Just thinking big makes you bigger.

00:28:57.700 --> 00:29:00.460
And what my point is just it takes just as
much effort.

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:04.120
I, my whole — and I learned again from bodybuilding.

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:08.690
From that kind of thing, I learned that don't
hold back.

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:13.620
When I went within age of 19, I was the youngest
Mr. Universe competitor.

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:17.850
I competed in a Mr. Universe contest and I
placed second.

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:19.840
I placed runner up.

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:24.780
So that a year later I went back with the
age of 20 and won Mr. Universe, the youngest

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:25.780
Mr. Universe ever.

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:27.740
But this is all because I was thinking big.

00:29:27.740 --> 00:29:30.840
I wasn't saying, "Oh, maybe in a few years
from now I go there."

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:32.780
Or "I shouldn't go there right now," or something.

00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:34.540
Or, "It's too early," or this.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:38.240
And then it's just third thing right away,
"I'm going to go for the second Miss Universe

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:39.240
next year.

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:40.270
I'm going to go to America.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:45.610
I'm going to go and make Joe Weider aware
of me and make sure that I win another competition

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:46.610
indoors."

00:29:46.610 --> 00:29:48.679
I was driven bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:29:48.679 --> 00:29:53.480
And even when I got into acting, I didn't
look at it as like, "I'm going to get some

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:55.679
character roles."

00:29:55.679 --> 00:29:57.900
I wasn't interested in character rolls.

00:29:57.900 --> 00:30:00.230
I wanted to be another Steve Reeves or Reg
Park.

00:30:00.230 --> 00:30:02.929
They were the stars of the Hercules movies.

00:30:02.929 --> 00:30:04.650
Clint Eastwood was the star.

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:08.220
We always Clint Eastwood in A Fistful of Dollars.

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:11.419
Clint Eastwood in [For] a Few Dollars More.

00:30:11.419 --> 00:30:14.860
Clint Eastwood in this movie, whatever it
was.

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:16.512
It was like, that's what I wanted.

00:30:16.512 --> 00:30:17.512
Charles Bronson.

00:30:17.512 --> 00:30:18.512
I want to be like Charles Bronson.

00:30:18.512 --> 00:30:20.430
I want to be like Warren Beatty.

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:22.490
I want to be like this guy.

00:30:22.490 --> 00:30:24.149
They were the top stars.

00:30:24.149 --> 00:30:25.950
And that's what I saw myself.

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:30.080
And they said, "Well, this ladder is very
hard to build or to climb up to."

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:32.840
I said, "Well, then I build my own ladder.

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:37.510
I build my own ladder and then I know exactly
how to get up there."

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:38.630
That's exactly what I did.

00:30:38.630 --> 00:30:41.289
I created my own way of getting up there.

00:30:41.289 --> 00:30:44.799
I took five hours that I learned in bodybuilding.

00:30:44.799 --> 00:30:50.840
I took five hours every day of working my
ass off to train and to train and to train

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:54.990
and to pose and to pose and to do all the
stuff that I needed to do.

00:30:54.990 --> 00:31:00.250
I said, "I'm going to do the same five hours,
but I'm going to go and learn English.

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:07.529
I'm going to learn acting, speech lessons,
voice lessons, accent remove lessons," or

00:31:07.529 --> 00:31:10.190
I should get my money back for those.

00:31:10.190 --> 00:31:17.030
But in any case, I took all of those lessons
one hour every day and I was grinding it out.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.250
And then I remember eventually it happened.

00:31:19.250 --> 00:31:28.000
People started hiring me, and the great thing
was that I felt that I should not be financially

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:29.480
vulnerable.

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:33.820
I first got into real estate and I worked
my ass off in real estate.

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:40.730
My first million I actually made in real estate
before I really got into acting.

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:44.270
And that helped me because now when they came
to me with stupid parts and says, "Do you

00:31:44.270 --> 00:31:45.270
want to play a bouncer?"

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:46.740
I said, "Fuck no.

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:49.080
Why would I play a bouncer?"

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:51.020
He said, "Well, what about a Nazi officer?

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:53.090
You have the German accent."

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:55.010
I said, "No, I don't want to be a Nazi officer."

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:57.190
I said, "I want to be a star.

00:31:57.190 --> 00:31:58.809
I want to be a leading man.

00:31:58.809 --> 00:32:00.360
I want to get rich and famous."

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:03.010
Just looking at these people, Charles Branson.

00:32:03.010 --> 00:32:05.010
And they said, "You're crazy.

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:06.010
It will never happen."

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:10.240
Well, I applied the other rule, which is don't
listen to the naysayers.

00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:12.510
I worked my ass off.

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:16.110
I did exactly what I did in the bodybuilding
I did in the movies.

00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:17.790
Eventually it happened.

00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:20.620
I started doing The Jayne Mansfield Story.

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:25.590
I started doing it with Kirk Douglas, Ann-Margret,
The Villain.

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:27.809
I was doing Streets of San Francisco.

00:32:27.809 --> 00:32:31.740
I was doing Stay Hungry and Pumping Iron,
all in the '70s.

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:35.100
And even with Lucille Ball, doing Happy Anniversary
and Goodbye.

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:37.260
I did all of those kind of things.

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:40.530
And then that led to the big role.

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:46.799
And now I've arrived, starring role in Conan
the Barbarian.

00:32:46.799 --> 00:32:54.159
When John Milius saw me, he says, "If we wouldn't
have Schwarzenegger, we would've had to build

00:32:54.159 --> 00:32:55.490
one."

00:32:55.490 --> 00:33:00.889
All of a sudden the body that everyone said
would never, ever become famous in the movies

00:33:00.889 --> 00:33:06.460
because the movies, no one has seen muscle
movies anymore.

00:33:06.460 --> 00:33:08.390
All of the opposite came true.

00:33:08.390 --> 00:33:10.419
My accent became very welcome.

00:33:10.419 --> 00:33:15.240
When they determinated they loved the German
accent, they did what Jim Cameron called,

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:18.140
"Schwarzenegger is talking like a machine.

00:33:18.140 --> 00:33:21.090
That's why it worked, like The Terminator."

00:33:21.090 --> 00:33:22.090
Things like that.

00:33:22.090 --> 00:33:27.269
All of a sudden the things that they said
would never make it in Hollywood, the accent,

00:33:27.269 --> 00:33:34.500
the name, the body, all of the things became
big pluses and it made it.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:37.700
That's my own ladder that I built.

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:39.380
That's why it's important.

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:43.519
Don't just worry about climbing a ladder that
someone else has built.

00:33:43.519 --> 00:33:45.899
No, build your own ladder.

00:33:45.899 --> 00:33:47.610
Just don't wait for anyone else.

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:48.610
That's what I did.

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:51.240
Tim Ferriss: Let me, I wanted to underscore
a few things for folks.

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:57.650
Number one, the building of many ladders was
not haphazard, it was systematic.

00:33:57.650 --> 00:34:02.970
You had the real estate as a financial buffer,
which gave you then the ability to pick your

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:03.970
shots.

00:34:03.970 --> 00:34:08.339
And you've been very good at doubling down
on betting on yourself in many different areas.

00:34:08.339 --> 00:34:16.409
But could you speak to Twins and what that
looked like with that particular film to bet

00:34:16.409 --> 00:34:17.409
on yourself?

00:34:17.409 --> 00:34:25.329
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Well, Twins was a little
bit out of nowhere because I had certain goals,

00:34:25.329 --> 00:34:29.750
but comedy was not one of my goals when I
got into movies.

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:32.720
And I felt like I can be funny in the movies.

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:35.980
In Conan, there were funny moments and all
that stuff.

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:46.230
But only when I started doing one action movie
after the next, my hunger, the whole philosophy

00:34:46.230 --> 00:34:50.369
of staying hungry, came out a little bit.

00:34:50.369 --> 00:34:56.179
And I said to myself, "Well, I wonder if we
ever could sell the idea of me doing a comedy."

00:34:56.179 --> 00:34:59.160
And then all of a sudden I started getting
obsessed with the idea.

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:00.829
And I started talking to everyone.

00:35:00.829 --> 00:35:03.050
I said, "Have you ever thought about me doing
a comedy?"

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:07.730
And of course, every studio executive said,
"Are you crazy, Arnold?

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:08.730
I mean, what do you think?

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:11.240
I'm making them millions of dollars of you
being an action hero.

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:15.849
You finally build you up to be this international
action hero, not only in America, but all

00:35:15.849 --> 00:35:17.020
over the world.

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:21.720
Why would I go start spending money on something
else that is not sure?

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:23.030
I love the action movies.

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:27.020
We're going to give you all the scripts for
action movies."

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:32.220
And so I said, "Yeah, but I understand, but
what about me doing an action movie for you

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:35.020
and then the next one we do is a comedy?"

00:35:35.020 --> 00:35:36.020
"No.

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:37.160
Why would I do that?

00:35:37.160 --> 00:35:38.220
You tell me.

00:35:38.220 --> 00:35:39.310
I mean, would you do it?"

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:40.310
I said, "Yeah."

00:35:40.310 --> 00:35:42.849
I said, "But here's the thing, what we need
to do."

00:35:42.849 --> 00:35:50.530
Then when we finally formed a partnership,
Danny DeVito, Ivan Reitman and myself, we

00:35:50.530 --> 00:35:54.700
got together and I said, "I can sympathize
with the studio.

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:56.270
Why would they take the risk?

00:35:56.270 --> 00:35:57.290
For what?

00:35:57.290 --> 00:36:00.040
Why don't we all take a risk?

00:36:00.040 --> 00:36:05.329
Why don't we go to them and say, instead of
us getting the big salaries, why don't we

00:36:05.329 --> 00:36:07.460
just say we do the movie for nothing?

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:09.760
Just give us a backend.

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:12.730
You don't have to pay us any salary whatsoever.

00:36:12.730 --> 00:36:18.920
If the production costs 16 and a half million
dollars, that's all you use.

00:36:18.920 --> 00:36:21.500
Not one penny more for us.

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:23.260
Fuck us.

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:25.320
Don't worry about us at all.

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:27.060
We have plenty of money.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:32.150
And if the movie goes in the toilet, we all
go in the toilet.

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:33.470
Everyone takes the risks.

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:35.050
Not just you, the studio.

00:36:35.050 --> 00:36:36.240
Wouldn't be fair, would it?"

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:41.319
They said, "Hey, this is my thinking, guys.

00:36:41.319 --> 00:36:42.319
You guys really good.

00:36:42.319 --> 00:36:43.660
What do you want in return?"

00:36:43.660 --> 00:36:52.569
We said, "All we want is just you give us
three 37.5 percent of ownership of the movie,

00:36:52.569 --> 00:36:54.880
and then we all go to the bank together.

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:57.820
If the movie goes through the roof, we all
make money.

00:36:57.820 --> 00:36:59.890
The movie goes in the toilet, none of us make
money."

00:36:59.890 --> 00:37:01.890
They said, "We are in."

00:37:01.890 --> 00:37:03.900
That's exactly what we did.

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:08.710
And it happened to be with Ivan Reitman's
genius directing.

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:16.490
And with Danny DeVito, great, great acting,
and everyone else around us, Kelly Preston

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:22.300
and everyone else, and me being involved,
we made the movie a huge hit.

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:29.119
As a matter of fact, that movie made more
money than any action movie made up until

00:37:29.119 --> 00:37:31.400
that point for me.

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:34.650
My action movie made always to 70, $80 million.

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:43.890
And that movie made $128 million domestically
and worldwide, $250 million.

00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:53.780
Now imagine the budget being 16 and half million
dollars, and your box office is 250 million.

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:59.260
Now we own 37 and a half, almost 40 percent
of that chunk.

00:37:59.260 --> 00:38:01.829
We all cleaned house.

00:38:01.829 --> 00:38:04.260
They were so fucking funny.

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:05.880
We'd go around.

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:13.130
As a matter of fact, Tom Pollock, who was
a fantastic studio leader and great producer

00:38:13.130 --> 00:38:21.260
and lawyer, he after the deal, he just basically
said, he went around the desk in his office.

00:38:21.260 --> 00:38:27.530
And he bent over and pulled out his pockets
and he says, "You guys fucked me and robbed

00:38:27.530 --> 00:38:28.970
me blind."

00:38:28.970 --> 00:38:34.160
And so, it was like we all were laughing because
we all were very good friends.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:35.160
He was right.

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:40.390
We really, because they were so worried about
the risk taking that we said, "We take the

00:38:40.390 --> 00:38:41.390
risk."

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:44.890
And sure enough, we did, and the risk paid
off.

00:38:44.890 --> 00:38:46.970
And so we just really cleaned the house.

00:38:46.970 --> 00:38:51.820
I made I think $70 million on Twins or something
like that in the end.

00:38:51.820 --> 00:38:56.400
And Danny made a fortune that he bought two
houses and built two houses.

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:59.430
We all got a lot of money, and Ivan Reitman.

00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:05.359
And so, that's what deals that we did then
in the future with Kindergarten Cop, we did

00:39:05.359 --> 00:39:06.890
it with Junior.

00:39:06.890 --> 00:39:10.930
It became a model that no one is going to
do today anymore.

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:14.750
The studios got smarter than that, so no one
was doing those deals anymore.

00:39:14.750 --> 00:39:17.540
But anyways, it was historic kind of a deal.

00:39:17.540 --> 00:39:23.180
But it was, I had the confidence that I could
pull it off, and Ivan had the confidence and

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:24.180
Danny had the confidence.

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:26.540
And so, together, we all did it.

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:30.710
And Universal Studios then had the confidence
and they promoted it really well.

00:39:30.710 --> 00:39:34.839
We hired Annie Leibovitz to do the photo shoot.

00:39:34.839 --> 00:39:40.310
And she took us on top of a bus against the
blue sky and just photographed Danny and me

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:42.200
leaning against each other.

00:39:42.200 --> 00:39:43.800
And that became the poster.

00:39:43.800 --> 00:39:46.290
And it was really genius.

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:50.829
Everyone worked together to make this a brilliant
movie and a successful movie.

00:39:50.829 --> 00:39:54.069
Tim Ferriss: This ties into "Sell, Sell, Sell."

00:39:54.069 --> 00:40:00.480
Why have an entire chapter/rule dedicated
to sell, sell, sell?

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:03.640
Because for a lot of people, they think of
selling as a dirty thing.

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:08.060
I don't happen to think of it that way, but
why is this so critical that "Sell, Sell,

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:11.090
Sell" would be one of the primary sections
in the book?

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:16.089
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Well, you can imagine
the reason why I called it "Sell, Sell, Sell"

00:40:16.089 --> 00:40:19.140
is because it does raise eyebrows.

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:22.950
It does make people say, "Wait a minute, selling
normally is a no-no."

00:40:22.950 --> 00:40:30.990
I mean, if you think about it that most of
the actors in the '70s and '80s refused to

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:31.990
sell their movies.

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:34.349
They said, "This is not my job.

00:40:34.349 --> 00:40:35.819
I'm an artist.

00:40:35.819 --> 00:40:36.880
I don't sell.

00:40:36.880 --> 00:40:39.609
I'm not a salesman out there," and all this
stuff.

00:40:39.609 --> 00:40:43.730
And I, this was my strength because I studied
selling.

00:40:43.730 --> 00:40:48.329
When I was in career education, I studied
to be a salesman.

00:40:48.329 --> 00:40:51.950
And so I realized then the importance of selling.

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:59.810
That no matter what you have — if you have
a podcast, if you have a movie, if you have

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:06.099
a painting, if you have a car, a technology,
a medicine, whatever it is — if people don't

00:41:06.099 --> 00:41:07.890
know about it, you have nothing.

00:41:07.890 --> 00:41:16.710
The more people that know about your product
or about your talent, the more you can go

00:41:16.710 --> 00:41:19.990
and be successful.

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:28.461
Therefore, this idea of selling, publicizing,
marketing, communicating, convincing, all

00:41:28.461 --> 00:41:31.640
of those kind of things is an art.

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:36.839
And there's art agencies that make millions
and millions of dollars to just figure out

00:41:36.839 --> 00:41:41.670
what the language should be in order to really
sell to the right people and have the right

00:41:41.670 --> 00:41:44.130
customers and sell a product the right way.

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:49.580
So it's an art to do that and I've learned
it way back when I was 15 years old.

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:51.420
I learned how to sell.

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:57.152
I remember when my boss said to me — he
says, "Now watch me carefully when I sell.

00:41:57.152 --> 00:41:59.020
There's this couple coming in."

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:05.079
This couple comes in and I worked in a store
that had wood products.

00:42:05.079 --> 00:42:07.380
It was like a lumber yard and the hardware
store.

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:09.850
It was like a hardware store type of a thing.

00:42:09.850 --> 00:42:13.859
This couple comes in, and they wanted to have
tiles.

00:42:13.859 --> 00:42:17.970
Immediately the guy started talking and then
my boss started talking to the guy and said,

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:19.200
"What kind of tiles do you want?

00:42:19.200 --> 00:42:22.610
Do you want the black tiles, the pink tiles,
the white tiles?"

00:42:22.610 --> 00:42:24.920
The guy said, "Well, I don't know."

00:42:24.920 --> 00:42:28.010
The woman said, "We want white tiles.

00:42:28.010 --> 00:42:30.619
And for the bathroom we want to have pink
tiles."

00:42:30.619 --> 00:42:33.470
The guy looked at her and he says, "Okay,
fine.

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:34.950
Let me take you over there."

00:42:34.950 --> 00:42:36.550
He said, "How much space?

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:37.550
How much tile do you need?"

00:42:37.550 --> 00:42:40.710
Again, the man didn't answer and the woman
answered.

00:42:40.710 --> 00:42:42.670
She says, "I need something [inaudible 00:55:25].

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:44.380
I've written down the measurements here.

00:42:44.380 --> 00:42:47.780
I need two meters by a meter 80 tall."

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:49.780
And blah, blah, blah.

00:42:49.780 --> 00:42:55.400
The guy then all of a sudden realized that
she's the customer.

00:42:55.400 --> 00:42:59.490
He started really paying attention to her
and asking all the questions and taking her

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:02.270
around, but included him also.

00:43:02.270 --> 00:43:07.480
And then on the end when they were satisfied
and we wrote up the order and we then told

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:11.160
him that they will be all delivered on Thursday.

00:43:11.160 --> 00:43:13.990
He came to me after they left, he said, "So
what did you learn?"

00:43:13.990 --> 00:43:19.630
I said, "Well," I said, "That you really sold
the tiles well and the colors and the difference

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:22.359
between real tiles and fake tiles and all
of this?"

00:43:22.359 --> 00:43:23.359
He says, "No, no.

00:43:23.359 --> 00:43:24.359
There was one other thing.

00:43:24.359 --> 00:43:28.960
I switched who I thought was the customer."

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:32.480
He says, "She was the customer, not him.

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:38.520
He paid for it, but she was the customer,
so I had to talk and address her because that

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:40.020
was the important thing.

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:41.980
She needed to be convinced.

00:43:41.980 --> 00:43:43.690
So I had to sell to her."

00:43:43.690 --> 00:43:50.609
And so I realized then that selling is an
art, that you have to improvise and adjust

00:43:50.609 --> 00:43:51.609
all the time.

00:43:51.609 --> 00:43:55.850
That if you go in front of a children audience,
for instance, you have to speak a totally

00:43:55.850 --> 00:44:00.530
different language when I talk in the class,
in the school, in afterschool programs, than

00:44:00.530 --> 00:44:03.900
I talk in Washington when I talk to legislators.

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:09.740
I have to talk to differently when I talk
to a crowd of fans at the movie theater than

00:44:09.740 --> 00:44:13.119
I talk to a bunch of lobbyists.

00:44:13.119 --> 00:44:14.140
It's always different.

00:44:14.140 --> 00:44:16.940
So you have to learn the art of selling.

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:19.190
And this is why selling is so important.

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:28.720
I remember that when Andy Warhol, when I was
being painted in his warehouse down in Soho,

00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:33.250
and Jamie Wyeth was there and Andy Warhol
was there.

00:44:33.250 --> 00:44:37.849
And that he always talked about that the most
important thing is that you don't just sell

00:44:37.849 --> 00:44:40.270
the art, but you sell yourself.

00:44:40.270 --> 00:44:41.840
You have to sell yourself.

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:50.150
You have to become an interesting person with
what parties you go to, who you hang out with,

00:44:50.150 --> 00:44:54.390
the photos that you take, the recordings that
you make, the magazine that you publish.

00:44:54.390 --> 00:45:00.829
All of this together makes me a character
and makes people fascinated to write about

00:45:00.829 --> 00:45:04.640
me, and therefore they write about my art.

00:45:04.640 --> 00:45:11.599
Sure enough, it worked because in no time
Andy Warhol's art became worth millions and

00:45:11.599 --> 00:45:12.970
millions of dollars.

00:45:12.970 --> 00:45:16.540
I used to buy it for $50,000, $30,000.

00:45:16.540 --> 00:45:23.970
I have the big Indian that is hanging in my
office that is now 10, $15 million.

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:26.809
I bought for $30,000.

00:45:26.809 --> 00:45:33.850
Imagine the value that Andy Warhol gained
by being just a different character, and being

00:45:33.850 --> 00:45:34.980
just strange.

00:45:34.980 --> 00:45:39.090
That people with a wig on it and the glasses
and all of these different things.

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:43.650
He ran around with a little tape recorder.

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:47.990
Tim Ferriss: What is the significance of shifting
gears?

00:45:47.990 --> 00:45:51.490
People can think of it, of course, in an automotive
capacity.

00:45:51.490 --> 00:45:55.930
But shift gears, what does that mean to you
and are there any particular stories that

00:45:55.930 --> 00:45:59.780
stand out related to that?

00:45:59.780 --> 00:46:01.570
Arnold Schwarzenegger: To give you an example.

00:46:01.570 --> 00:46:03.220
I talked about it earlier.

00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:07.420
You wake up from a surgery that you think
is two hours, they replace your heart valve

00:46:07.420 --> 00:46:13.160
and then someone tells you, "We poked through
your heart wall and you now have been out

00:46:13.160 --> 00:46:20.020
for 16 hours and now you have to stay here
six days or seven days, and you're not out

00:46:20.020 --> 00:46:21.559
of the woods yet.

00:46:21.559 --> 00:46:26.579
We have to do everything we can to keep you
alive and you almost died on the operating

00:46:26.579 --> 00:46:27.579
table.

00:46:27.579 --> 00:46:33.109
And it could still happen next night if you
don't really get going with the walking and

00:46:33.109 --> 00:46:34.109
if you get pneumonia."

00:46:34.109 --> 00:46:36.710
So that, you have to shift gears very quickly.

00:46:36.710 --> 00:46:37.710
So this is the art of what I call the art
of improvisation.

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:39.340
I usually call it the art of improvisation.

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:43.720
That you have to be very good in improvising
because there's a lot of things that come

00:46:43.720 --> 00:46:48.230
up to you in life but you have to be really
good in improvising.

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:50.480
And this happened to me all the time when
I was governor.

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:52.930
I had to quickly shift gears.

00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:58.730
And it happened also in show business where
you have to shift gears where the unexpected

00:46:58.730 --> 00:47:03.829
is happening and you have to be ready for
that and confront that.

00:47:03.829 --> 00:47:05.869
That's the most important thing.

00:47:05.869 --> 00:47:12.930
So it's just so many people get stuck on certain
things and the track and they then cannot

00:47:12.930 --> 00:47:14.339
get off that track.

00:47:14.339 --> 00:47:19.430
I just always felt like I was very good in
shifting gears.

00:47:19.430 --> 00:47:24.300
Like going from bodybuilding to show business.

00:47:24.300 --> 00:47:29.430
You really had to shift gears because all
of a sudden certain other things became important.

00:47:29.430 --> 00:47:32.330
Think about it, you go and you do bodybuilding.

00:47:32.330 --> 00:47:37.670
Every athlete always tells you that you got
to go and keep the emotions out of the way

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:39.641
because it's the emotions that's going to
kill you.

00:47:39.641 --> 00:47:46.869
You cannot go and train and compete and train
for a competition for a world championship

00:47:46.869 --> 00:47:53.040
or the Olympic games or whatever it is and
be emotionally involved in whatever it is.

00:47:53.040 --> 00:47:54.119
Because it can derail you.

00:47:54.119 --> 00:47:57.900
So, okay, so you do that and I become a master
in that.

00:47:57.900 --> 00:47:59.740
I became cornerstone.

00:47:59.740 --> 00:48:11.600
But then all of a sudden you go and you start
taking acting classes and you start hearing

00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:19.990
from the acting teacher, "Arnold, you talk
like a fucking cold fish.

00:48:19.990 --> 00:48:22.980
There's no emotions there."

00:48:22.980 --> 00:48:26.871
I've got to go and find the emotions.

00:48:26.871 --> 00:48:28.119
So think about that for a second.

00:48:28.119 --> 00:48:35.170
All your life long you hear now that this
is bad and now all of a sudden you hear you

00:48:35.170 --> 00:48:36.630
have to be more emotional.

00:48:36.630 --> 00:48:40.720
You have to be in touch with your emotions.

00:48:40.720 --> 00:48:45.970
"Have you thought about lately the smell of
a rose?"

00:48:45.970 --> 00:48:49.160
I said, "The smell of what?"

00:48:49.160 --> 00:48:51.520
"A rose."

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:57.840
He says, "A rose smells a certain way, it's
a beautiful smell."

00:48:57.840 --> 00:49:01.320
I said, "What does this have to do with acting?"

00:49:01.320 --> 00:49:02.440
He says, "Ah."

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:10.950
He says, "If you sit there in the scene and
you start thinking about that smell of a rose,

00:49:10.950 --> 00:49:13.380
you have a totally different facial expression.

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:15.900
You close the eyes.

00:49:15.900 --> 00:49:20.549
Let's assume that you want to compliment the
woman, the perfume she wears.

00:49:20.549 --> 00:49:26.059
You can't go and say, 'I like your perfume.'

00:49:26.059 --> 00:49:27.309
Stupid.

00:49:27.309 --> 00:49:35.950
But if you go and say, 'What are you wearing?

00:49:35.950 --> 00:49:43.090
Oh, it smells wonderful,' you have good taste."

00:49:43.090 --> 00:49:45.580
He says, "That's a totally different tone."

00:49:45.580 --> 00:49:46.790
He says, "It would change your voice.

00:49:46.790 --> 00:49:50.410
If you smell the rose, it would change your
voice.

00:49:50.410 --> 00:49:55.069
But you only can do that if you really smell
it and be in touch with that."

00:49:55.069 --> 00:49:58.050
So that's what I'm talking about shifting
gears very quickly.

00:49:58.050 --> 00:50:04.630
So from one year to the next, I had to all
of a sudden have all the emotions kick in.

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:09.220
Make everything work that didn't really work
in the past.

00:50:09.220 --> 00:50:17.020
Tim Ferriss: So I'm curious to ask you about
how that re-accessing of emotion maybe has

00:50:17.020 --> 00:50:23.430
informed how you experience grief yourself.

00:50:23.430 --> 00:50:28.230
Since we last spoke, you lost Franco, Franco
Columbu, and I'm just wondering what that

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:30.260
grief was like for you to experience.

00:50:30.260 --> 00:50:33.920
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Well, I have to say
I react a little bit differently to those

00:50:33.920 --> 00:50:46.369
kinds of things than everyone because to me
it's not so much the shock as it is the ongoing

00:50:46.369 --> 00:50:51.860
missing a person.

00:50:51.860 --> 00:50:58.040
Because there's certain friends that have
become part of you.

00:50:58.040 --> 00:51:05.579
And so if they pass away and they die, something
dies in you.

00:51:05.579 --> 00:51:14.570
And so when I imagine every day when I walk
into my living room and I see this chess board

00:51:14.570 --> 00:51:23.690
where Franco and I played chess in the last
10 years, two, three times a week and drank

00:51:23.690 --> 00:51:28.350
wine, smoked [inaudible 01:15:06] and just
talked.

00:51:28.350 --> 00:51:36.849
Talked like 65- and 70-year-olds talk, rather
than the way 20-year-olds talk, like in the

00:51:36.849 --> 00:51:37.849
old days.

00:51:37.849 --> 00:51:41.040
And because Franco, I've known since I was
18 years old.

00:51:41.040 --> 00:51:47.260
So then our conversations were differently
than they were as of the last 10 years where

00:51:47.260 --> 00:51:52.040
we talk about kids, where we talk about family,
where we talk about where we grew up and about

00:51:52.040 --> 00:51:59.710
the past and more deeper conversations and
more emotional conversations.

00:51:59.710 --> 00:52:03.729
And now all of a sudden, you're — every
day when you walk into your living room, you

00:52:03.729 --> 00:52:08.370
see this chest table down the corner, but
Franco is not sitting there anymore.

00:52:08.370 --> 00:52:11.839
That, to me, is heartbreaking.

00:52:11.839 --> 00:52:21.299
And when I go to the gym in the drive down
with the bicycle and Franco came on the bicycle,

00:52:21.299 --> 00:52:23.170
he was not good in bicycling riding.

00:52:23.170 --> 00:52:24.510
He was all over the place.

00:52:24.510 --> 00:52:26.309
So that was funny.

00:52:26.309 --> 00:52:32.340
And I had people sometimes videotaping just
to show how goofy he looks on a bicycle.

00:52:32.340 --> 00:52:37.280
I think the bike seat was maybe too high up,
but we couldn't know what the problem was.

00:52:37.280 --> 00:52:40.800
It was just hilarious because he was 5'3,
according to him.

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:44.400
I think he was 5'1 or 5'2 at the most.

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:46.890
But anyway, he always said 5'3.

00:52:46.890 --> 00:52:50.630
And then working out with him, the fun of
working out with him.

00:52:50.630 --> 00:52:53.500
Then I had him in so many movies.

00:52:53.500 --> 00:53:02.280
I remember when I directed the movie The Switch,
for Tales [from] the Crypt, I had him in there

00:53:02.280 --> 00:53:06.430
Conan the Barbarian, I had him in Terminator.

00:53:06.430 --> 00:53:08.540
I had him in all those movies in there.

00:53:08.540 --> 00:53:11.819
So he became kind of part of me.

00:53:11.819 --> 00:53:17.720
So to me, it's not just the initial shock
when someone tells you, "Oh, Franco just passed

00:53:17.720 --> 00:53:20.010
away on the beach in Sardinia."

00:53:20.010 --> 00:53:26.750
It is also then a daily thing, a weekly thing
amongst this.

00:53:26.750 --> 00:53:34.059
Every day, every time I go to the Arnold Classic
and we hand out the — I have a trophy that

00:53:34.059 --> 00:53:40.100
is the Franco Columbu posing trophy of most
muscular man trophy.

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:46.510
And we hand those out with Franco's body on
it that got made by your great Italian sculptor,

00:53:46.510 --> 00:53:49.040
the double bicep pose.

00:53:49.040 --> 00:53:59.890
And the double bicep pose intentionally because
Franco was really never known for his biceps.

00:53:59.890 --> 00:54:08.619
Because he was known — he had such overpowering
back, his lats, his chest, his deltoids.

00:54:08.619 --> 00:54:13.790
It was so overpowering that people sometimes
didn't even see the arms.

00:54:13.790 --> 00:54:19.180
So I, on purpose, wanted to do a double bicep
pose so in the future, people also remember

00:54:19.180 --> 00:54:20.180
him for his biceps.

00:54:20.180 --> 00:54:23.520
But it's just a great, great sculpture.

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:26.230
And so, to me, Franco will live on forever.

00:54:26.230 --> 00:54:32.090
So is Joe [inaudible 01:18:15] on Ben [inaudible
01:18:16] and Dave Draper and Sergio Oliva

00:54:32.090 --> 00:54:34.319
and Bill Pearl and Reg Park.

00:54:34.319 --> 00:54:40.240
To me, I see them all sitting in front of
me when there's the Arnold Classic.

00:54:40.240 --> 00:54:44.920
And I see them all sitting there laughing
and having a great time and watching the Arnold

00:54:44.920 --> 00:54:51.089
Classic and watching how body putting is progressing,
how the cash prices are going up, how we have

00:54:51.089 --> 00:54:57.160
bigger and bigger sponsors, how we have a
bigger and bigger convention and expo and

00:54:57.160 --> 00:54:58.160
all of this.

00:54:58.160 --> 00:54:59.290
How they enjoy all that.

00:54:59.290 --> 00:55:01.410
So that's what I see out there now.

00:55:01.410 --> 00:55:08.170
But it's in between kind of like, should I
have tears in my eyes when I'm out there and

00:55:08.170 --> 00:55:16.421
looking at all those faces of those bodybuilding
champions and promoters of bodybuilding, or

00:55:16.421 --> 00:55:17.910
should I smile?

00:55:17.910 --> 00:55:19.569
It's this combination of things.

00:55:19.569 --> 00:55:25.440
Tim Ferriss: I would love to get your thoughts
on aging and relating to aging, because a

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:28.930
lot of people struggle with relating or thinking
about aging.

00:55:28.930 --> 00:55:32.809
I, for the first time in the last nine months
have had chronic pain for the first time due

00:55:32.809 --> 00:55:37.990
to a spinal issue, which is the first time
in my life I've ever experienced that.

00:55:37.990 --> 00:55:43.559
And I'm wondering if you could share anything
about what you've learned or decided with

00:55:43.559 --> 00:55:47.700
respect to aging, just getting older as we
all do.

00:55:47.700 --> 00:55:52.690
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Well, the first time
at all I experienced something similar to

00:55:52.690 --> 00:55:56.520
that is when I had my open heart surgery.

00:55:56.520 --> 00:55:59.319
I was not even 50 years old.

00:55:59.319 --> 00:56:03.030
It was in April and in July I'm 50.

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:06.799
So it was just a few months before I was 50.

00:56:06.799 --> 00:56:11.690
And it was the first time where I woke up
after two heart surgeries.

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:18.050
The first one didn't work so they did the
second one within 48 hours.

00:56:18.050 --> 00:56:26.210
And after that, I felt like I was damaged
goods.

00:56:26.210 --> 00:56:30.520
I didn't feel any more invincible.

00:56:30.520 --> 00:56:33.520
I didn't feel like I can handle anything.

00:56:33.520 --> 00:56:44.380
All of a sudden there were limits put on me
where the doctor said, "Don't train as heavy.

00:56:44.380 --> 00:56:50.819
Every time you force your reps, you put pressure
on your valve unnecessarily.

00:56:50.819 --> 00:56:58.480
We have to replace those valves again in maybe
10, 15 years from now.

00:56:58.480 --> 00:57:01.520
So the more you put pressure on it, the faster
we will have to.

00:57:01.520 --> 00:57:03.119
It's like a tire.

00:57:03.119 --> 00:57:05.119
It's 30,000 miles per tire.

00:57:05.119 --> 00:57:08.500
You can use it up in one year, you can use
it up in 20 years.

00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:10.630
It's up to you."

00:57:10.630 --> 00:57:13.270
It's that a thing.

00:57:13.270 --> 00:57:18.470
It was the first time where I started thinking
about when I did stunts.

00:57:18.470 --> 00:57:29.089
I remember that, right after that, was a stunt
in End of Days, where the woman that was possessed

00:57:29.089 --> 00:57:38.580
by the Devil takes the piano and runs it against
my chest, wanted to kill me.

00:57:38.580 --> 00:57:46.250
And normally, you can run a piano into my
chest, doesn't make any fucking difference,

00:57:46.250 --> 00:57:48.050
I don't care.

00:57:48.050 --> 00:57:54.390
But because of the heart surgery, and having
been, now, cut open in the chest, I did not

00:57:54.390 --> 00:57:57.859
know how vulnerable the rib cage is.

00:57:57.859 --> 00:58:03.599
I told them to measure out the distance with
a rope.

00:58:03.599 --> 00:58:06.069
And then, the rope comes to an end.

00:58:06.069 --> 00:58:12.539
And it stops, an eighth, or a quarter of an
inch, before my chest.

00:58:12.539 --> 00:58:22.260
It looks, still, like it's smashing foot in
the ice-cold solid, but you start planning

00:58:22.260 --> 00:58:25.359
on your vulnerability.

00:58:25.359 --> 00:58:33.490
And this then continues on, because all of
a sudden — you used to hop up stairs, and

00:58:33.490 --> 00:58:39.539
hop down stairs, in a squatting position,
to just get out of breath, so that when you

00:58:39.539 --> 00:58:42.020
start a scene, you're exhausted.

00:58:42.020 --> 00:58:49.230
You're cutting in, in the middle of a fight
scene, so that you're — and you have to

00:58:49.230 --> 00:58:51.369
— big breath.

00:58:51.369 --> 00:58:58.690
All of a sudden, you start jumping up and
down, and your knees start hurting.

00:58:58.690 --> 00:59:00.640
Now, you realize, "Okay.

00:59:00.640 --> 00:59:08.510
Between 50 and 60, it's the knee punishment,
so I have to watch my knees."

00:59:08.510 --> 00:59:15.369
Then after you're 70, you said, for the first
time, saying, "I noticed myself walking less.

00:59:15.369 --> 00:59:18.210
Why am I walking less?

00:59:18.210 --> 00:59:23.270
I used to love hiking, four or five hours,
up the steep mountain, and all of this I love,

00:59:23.270 --> 00:59:25.700
why am I walking less?"

00:59:25.700 --> 00:59:33.160
Then I realized that I got back pain when
I walk a long time.

00:59:33.160 --> 00:59:38.210
I started getting cramps in my back, so I
started walking less.

00:59:38.210 --> 00:59:44.220
I started doing stretching exercises for the
back.

00:59:44.220 --> 00:59:45.920
Things like that start creeping up.

00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:48.832
Then you have to start, really — be disciplined
and say, "Okay.

00:59:48.832 --> 00:59:52.660
I still have to walk so many miles a day,
so many steps a day."

00:59:52.660 --> 00:59:54.079
Blah, blah, blah.

00:59:54.079 --> 00:59:57.200
But now, you have to make yourself what came,
normally, natural.

00:59:57.200 --> 00:59:58.470
You get one thing after the next.

00:59:58.470 --> 01:00:01.539
Then you have shoulder surgeries in both shoulders.

01:00:01.539 --> 01:00:09.410
Like yesterday, I had elbow surgery, because
my nerve had to be relocated.

01:00:09.410 --> 01:00:15.740
Because, where the nerve was, it created pressure
on my nerve.

01:00:15.740 --> 01:00:19.490
And therefore, my little fingers started getting
numb.

01:00:19.490 --> 01:00:22.780
Now, that comes in — it's in the late 70s.

01:00:22.780 --> 01:00:26.510
[inaudible 01:24:28] All of a sudden, what's
up with nerves?

01:00:26.510 --> 01:00:32.599
And someone talks to you about the neuropathy,
about your legs and feet.

01:00:32.599 --> 01:00:37.109
And this is how it just creeps up on you,
all this stuff.

01:00:37.109 --> 01:00:42.380
And the interesting thing is, it's like weightlifting.

01:00:42.380 --> 01:00:47.329
It doesn't matter how old you are, or how
young you are.

01:00:47.329 --> 01:00:49.420
It doesn't matter how rich you are.

01:00:49.420 --> 01:00:51.500
It doesn't matter what color you are.

01:00:51.500 --> 01:00:54.760
It doesn't matter from where you're from.

01:00:54.760 --> 01:00:59.100
200 pounds is 200 pounds.

01:00:59.100 --> 01:01:03.020
It's the same fucking thing for everybody.

01:01:03.020 --> 01:01:05.600
And the same as, also, when you get older.

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:07.950
It makes no fucking difference who you are.

01:01:07.950 --> 01:01:13.420
You can be the biggest celebrity in the world,
but you still get your back pain.

01:01:13.420 --> 01:01:15.130
You still get your hip pain.

01:01:15.130 --> 01:01:17.089
You still get your shoulder pain.

01:01:17.089 --> 01:01:19.020
You still get your elbow pain.

01:01:19.020 --> 01:01:20.069
You still get numb fingers.

01:01:20.069 --> 01:01:21.500
You still have to watch your heart.

01:01:21.500 --> 01:01:23.579
You still have to watch the diet.

01:01:23.579 --> 01:01:25.510
You still get fat if you don't watch the diet.

01:01:25.510 --> 01:01:30.599
If you eat three times a day, a full meal,
you get fat, you have to cut one meal out.

01:01:30.599 --> 01:01:33.290
All of this kind of stuff you have to start
doing.

01:01:33.290 --> 01:01:35.260
It's just that simple.

01:01:35.260 --> 01:01:42.000
And this is all so that we stay alive longer.

01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:46.109
The time you're born, your time cut is set.

01:01:46.109 --> 01:01:48.039
It's set.

01:01:48.039 --> 01:01:52.190
The only thing that changes it is you, right?

01:01:52.190 --> 01:01:53.510
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

01:01:53.510 --> 01:01:57.869
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Let's assume I'm set
for 85.

01:01:57.869 --> 01:02:01.619
I can decide, do I want to go to 90?

01:02:01.619 --> 01:02:07.579
Yeah, I can do that, but then you have to
live really healthy.

01:02:07.579 --> 01:02:11.260
Someone else has said, for 90, you can live
to 100.

01:02:11.260 --> 01:02:14.579
You can stretch it a little bit.

01:02:14.579 --> 01:02:18.660
And you can also fuck it up big time.

01:02:18.660 --> 01:02:22.500
You can be set for 85, and be wiped out with
70.

01:02:22.500 --> 01:02:25.360
My dad wiped out with 66.

01:02:25.360 --> 01:02:29.099
He was in pension for one year, and then he
wiped out.

01:02:29.099 --> 01:02:34.089
He died because of too much smoking and alcohol,
and all of those things.

01:02:34.089 --> 01:02:35.559
He cut himself short.

01:02:35.559 --> 01:02:40.880
He maybe was meant to be 80, but he definitely
wiped out with the 66.

01:02:40.880 --> 01:02:47.150
My mother, she died with 76.

01:02:47.150 --> 01:02:53.051
She did it herself, because she had a congenital
heart disease, which is what I have, which

01:02:53.051 --> 01:02:54.051
is the valve.

01:02:54.051 --> 01:02:57.500
But she had the choice to get surgery or not.

01:02:57.500 --> 01:02:58.869
She says, "No.

01:02:58.869 --> 01:03:02.520
If God wants me, he should have me."

01:03:02.520 --> 01:03:08.069
She resisted any surgery.

01:03:08.069 --> 01:03:13.220
There's some people, when you watch the shows,
in Sardinia, they live to 100.

01:03:13.220 --> 01:03:16.270
Because they have no problems.

01:03:16.270 --> 01:03:18.990
They sleep in the afternoon.

01:03:18.990 --> 01:03:21.280
They take their naps.

01:03:21.280 --> 01:03:23.000
They eat well.

01:03:23.000 --> 01:03:25.339
They walk around for miles and miles every
day.

01:03:25.339 --> 01:03:27.420
They still walk and work.

01:03:27.420 --> 01:03:33.809
The women are still in the kitchen, with the
age of 90, making food for the whole family,

01:03:33.809 --> 01:03:34.869
and all this stuff.

01:03:34.869 --> 01:03:35.869
They push it.

01:03:35.869 --> 01:03:38.779
They push the [inaudible 01:27:44] beyond
of what they were meant for.

01:03:38.779 --> 01:03:40.830
This is the way we can do it.

01:03:40.830 --> 01:03:47.010
But I think there is a reason, when you get
to certain age, to be concerned about it.

01:03:47.010 --> 01:03:50.470
I don't know if it's the age that you're in
now.

01:03:50.470 --> 01:03:52.140
You're still a young punk.

01:03:52.140 --> 01:03:59.200
Put them in — your whole life earlier, you
could be my grandson.

01:03:59.200 --> 01:04:09.109
Tim Ferriss: You've been an athlete, as it
was laid out in the three chapters in the

01:04:09.109 --> 01:04:10.109
miniseries.

01:04:10.109 --> 01:04:11.109
Athlete, actor, American.

01:04:11.109 --> 01:04:12.440
You've had this arc.

01:04:12.440 --> 01:04:15.500
How do you think of yourself now?

01:04:15.500 --> 01:04:17.770
What is your identity now?

01:04:17.770 --> 01:04:21.920
And how do you hope to use the time that you
have left?

01:04:21.920 --> 01:04:24.740
Of course, you have this book, Be Useful:
Seven Tools for Life.

01:04:24.740 --> 01:04:27.049
You have the newsletter, which has done very
well.

01:04:27.049 --> 01:04:28.970
You have more than half a million people for
Pump Club.

01:04:28.970 --> 01:04:30.260
You have the Pump app.

01:04:30.260 --> 01:04:33.900
But how do you think of yourself now?

01:04:33.900 --> 01:04:40.060
And what do you want to focus on, in the next
10 years, if you have, let's say, 10 years

01:04:40.060 --> 01:04:41.180
left, something like that?

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:47.520
Arnold Schwarzenegger: I don't really think
of myself, now, any different than I thought

01:04:47.520 --> 01:04:52.760
of myself when I was governor, when I thought
of myself as an actor, as a bodybuilder.

01:04:52.760 --> 01:04:58.510
I'm very rarely in the moment, where I just
appreciate what I'm doing right now, because

01:04:58.510 --> 01:05:00.930
I always think about the future.

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:02.940
I don't like the past.

01:05:02.940 --> 01:05:06.299
I appreciate the presence, but I really live
for the future.

01:05:06.299 --> 01:05:09.180
I always just live about where I want to go.

01:05:09.180 --> 01:05:12.220
Because there's a lot of things I want to
accomplish environmentally.

01:05:12.220 --> 01:05:15.819
There's a lot of things I want to accomplish
when it comes to public policy.

01:05:15.819 --> 01:05:18.440
There's a lot of things I want to accomplish
in show business.

01:05:18.440 --> 01:05:23.390
There's a lot of things that I want to accomplish
in the promotion of health and fitness, and

01:05:23.390 --> 01:05:24.390
bodybuilding.

01:05:24.390 --> 01:05:30.700
All of those different worlds I, hopefully,
can manage, to combine them and create a certain

01:05:30.700 --> 01:05:34.710
synergy, some words that [inaudible 01:29:40]
one can help from the other, so that bodybuilding

01:05:34.710 --> 01:05:39.700
can help — from the show business, my success
in show business, that the show business can

01:05:39.700 --> 01:05:43.369
get helped with the success of the fitness
movement.

01:05:43.369 --> 01:05:49.810
Then my newsletter that I have, which is going
through the roof right now, the Pump Club,

01:05:49.810 --> 01:05:52.700
all that is playing into this whole thing.

01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:59.640
I'm very happy that, all of a sudden, now,
it is like an unexpected new era for me, which

01:05:59.640 --> 01:06:05.640
is the era of motivational speeches, the era
of motivational books, the era of motivational

01:06:05.640 --> 01:06:06.829
newsletters.

01:06:06.829 --> 01:06:12.670
Not in my wildest dreams did I ever think
about they don't want to create a positive

01:06:12.670 --> 01:06:13.799
corner on the internet.

01:06:13.799 --> 01:06:18.520
And that was only because there was so much
negativity out there.

01:06:18.520 --> 01:06:22.869
I started thinking about, "Maybe I should
say some nice things, and some positive things."

01:06:22.869 --> 01:06:26.869
And it became a huge hit beyond my expectation.

01:06:26.869 --> 01:06:29.290
Now, I do speeches every so often.

01:06:29.290 --> 01:06:35.970
If it's the Ukrainian war, the Russian won
Ukraine, or if it is the insurrection, or

01:06:35.970 --> 01:06:41.520
if it is prejudice, or whatever the issues
are, I tackle those.

01:06:41.520 --> 01:06:47.220
As I said to you earlier, I'm the guy that
climbs up Mount Everest and sees another bunch

01:06:47.220 --> 01:06:48.680
of peaks.

01:06:48.680 --> 01:06:52.670
And therefore, I say, "Oh, my God, I didn't
even know they were there."

01:06:52.670 --> 01:06:53.670
And I climb them.

01:06:53.670 --> 01:06:55.420
That's what I do, just a continuous climb.

01:06:55.420 --> 01:06:56.420
Nothing changes.

01:06:56.420 --> 01:06:57.990
I would climb to 20.

01:06:57.990 --> 01:06:59.300
I'm climbing now.

01:06:59.300 --> 01:07:05.270
Tim Ferriss: What do you hope the impact of
Be Useful will be?

01:07:05.270 --> 01:07:12.230
What would you hope people to gain from it,
or use it for?

01:07:12.230 --> 01:07:17.900
Arnold Schwarzenegger: I think that the whole
thing is about helping people live a better

01:07:17.900 --> 01:07:24.110
life, and be able to fulfill their dreams,
whatever those dreams are.

01:07:24.110 --> 01:07:26.240
It's just simple things like that.

01:07:26.240 --> 01:07:30.400
Don't listen to the naysayers, or create a
vision.

01:07:30.400 --> 01:07:34.779
And I know that, because I ask my kids, when
they were 18, 19 years old, "What do you want

01:07:34.779 --> 01:07:35.779
to do?

01:07:35.779 --> 01:07:36.779
Why do you want to go to college?"

01:07:36.779 --> 01:07:38.299
They couldn't answer me.

01:07:38.299 --> 01:07:42.349
I could answer that question when I was 18,
19.

01:07:42.349 --> 01:07:46.780
I'm concerned about that, because I said to
myself, they're looking too much in the computer,

01:07:46.780 --> 01:07:50.599
too much on the iPhone, and on the iPad, and
they get ideas from someone else.

01:07:50.599 --> 01:07:54.320
But this is their ideas, but not their ideas,
not my kids' ideas.

01:07:54.320 --> 01:07:58.940
They need to be by themselves, and sit in
the jacuzzi, or sit in somewhere in the mountain,

01:07:58.940 --> 01:08:01.309
or out there by themselves, and start thinking.

01:08:01.309 --> 01:08:04.710
Let the dreams come into your mind.

01:08:04.710 --> 01:08:10.010
Let the deep inside come out, and give yourself
time.

01:08:10.010 --> 01:08:12.339
Don't always look at the machine.

01:08:12.339 --> 01:08:19.900
I'm trying to tell people, these are simple
rules, that I talk about in the book, where

01:08:19.900 --> 01:08:24.330
you learn, that here's how I create a goal.

01:08:24.330 --> 01:08:26.980
Because without a goal, without a vision,
you have nothing.

01:08:26.980 --> 01:08:28.370
Where are you going to go?

01:08:28.370 --> 01:08:35.500
It's like you have an airplane pilot that
doesn't know where to fly, and he has the

01:08:35.500 --> 01:08:36.500
best airplane.

01:08:36.500 --> 01:08:39.250
You can fly around, around, around, and it'll
eventually crash.

01:08:39.250 --> 01:08:41.460
That's what happens to you in your life.

01:08:41.460 --> 01:08:42.460
You crash.

01:08:42.460 --> 01:08:44.330
You're not going to go anywhere.

01:08:44.330 --> 01:08:45.900
You need to have a direction.

01:08:45.900 --> 01:08:47.270
You need to have a goal.

01:08:47.270 --> 01:08:48.549
Why you get up in the morning?

01:08:48.549 --> 01:08:50.580
What are you struggling towards?

01:08:50.580 --> 01:08:51.580
What it is.

01:08:51.580 --> 01:08:55.449
Why are you happy to go to bed at night, if
you need some rest, to get up in the next

01:08:55.449 --> 01:08:57.009
morning, and have the energy again?

01:08:57.009 --> 01:08:58.449
All of this has to have purpose.

01:08:58.449 --> 01:09:00.909
There has to be purpose.

01:09:00.909 --> 01:09:05.859
This is what I try to do, is kindle a little
bit of this light in my book, and say to people,

01:09:05.859 --> 01:09:07.909
in a casual way, "Have a goal.

01:09:07.909 --> 01:09:09.259
Here's how you can do it.

01:09:09.259 --> 01:09:13.850
This is how I did it."

01:09:13.850 --> 01:09:16.060
Don't be afraid of big course.

01:09:16.060 --> 01:09:20.420
Big course are just as easy as little course
is.

01:09:20.420 --> 01:09:25.710
By the way, I know that every human being
is afraid of failure.

01:09:25.710 --> 01:09:29.200
But you can overcome that by accepting failure.

01:09:29.200 --> 01:09:37.219
In bodybuilding, we go until failure, with
our reps.

01:09:37.219 --> 01:09:40.089
Every single day, when we train, we experience
failure.

01:09:40.089 --> 01:09:45.000
We are not afraid of it because — like Muhammad
Ali said, he said, "Hey…” —I used to

01:09:45.000 --> 01:09:46.260
be in the stadium.

01:09:46.260 --> 01:09:49.380
I said, "How many reps do you do in sit-ups?"

01:09:49.380 --> 01:09:55.270
He says, "I don't start counting until it
starts hurting, I start failing.

01:09:55.270 --> 01:09:58.949
That's when I start counting."

01:09:58.949 --> 01:10:05.960
In lifting, you can only know how much you
lift if you're willing to fail.

01:10:05.960 --> 01:10:11.489
Michael Jordan, when he talked about his 5,000
shots that he missed in basketball, and, how

01:10:11.489 --> 01:10:15.830
many, 280 some games he missed in basketball,
and all of this stuff, "That's what made me

01:10:15.830 --> 01:10:16.830
great."

01:10:16.830 --> 01:10:18.980
Wow.

01:10:18.980 --> 01:10:21.370
That's an eye-opener, when you hear that.

01:10:21.370 --> 01:10:22.930
That's really powerful.

01:10:22.930 --> 01:10:28.580
The greatest basketball player talks about
failure that made him great.

01:10:28.580 --> 01:10:30.449
People should look at that.

01:10:30.449 --> 01:10:33.210
They should start thinking about that.

01:10:33.210 --> 01:10:36.730
Don't start approaching everything with, "Oh,
I'm afraid.

01:10:36.730 --> 01:10:38.240
What if I fail?

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:40.530
What if he doesn't like it?

01:10:40.530 --> 01:10:43.280
What if I make a fool of myself?"

01:10:43.280 --> 01:10:46.190
People are afraid of speaking.

01:10:46.190 --> 01:10:50.580
Public speaking is the biggest fear that people
have, because they're worried that they may

01:10:50.580 --> 01:10:53.060
fail or sound stupid, and stuff like that.

01:10:53.060 --> 01:10:54.080
You get all that.

01:10:54.080 --> 01:10:59.280
Better get rid of all of this, and the worry
about failure, and you will be, then, free.

01:10:59.280 --> 01:11:02.840
And I'm not saying you will be able to get
rid of it completely.

01:11:02.840 --> 01:11:07.280
You can never change 100 percent, but you
can change somewhat, so that you're not as

01:11:07.280 --> 01:11:10.801
afraid, anymore, of failure, and that you're
actually looking forward to that, and that

01:11:10.801 --> 01:11:14.420
you're saying, "Okay, I'm going to go out
and do a failure."

01:11:14.420 --> 01:11:19.239
That you approach it differently than the
way you look at failure.

01:11:19.239 --> 01:11:25.719
Look, I've always pushed myself — "What
do you think when you run for governor?"

01:11:25.719 --> 01:11:28.800
It would be the highest embarrassment if you
would've lost, right?

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:29.800
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

01:11:29.800 --> 01:11:31.239
Arnold Schwarzenegger: But I took the chance.

01:11:31.239 --> 01:11:33.040
I was not afraid of failure.

01:11:33.040 --> 01:11:34.470
I could see my vision very clearly.

01:11:34.470 --> 01:11:35.470
[inaudible 01:40:35].

01:11:35.470 --> 01:11:36.470
I can see my vision very clearly.

01:11:36.470 --> 01:11:41.370
"This is how I'm going to sell to the California
people, what I'm going to do for Californians.

01:11:41.370 --> 01:11:44.390
And that's how I'm going to approach the governorship."

01:11:44.390 --> 01:11:46.039
And blah, blah, blah.

01:11:46.039 --> 01:11:47.600
And if they buy in, great.

01:11:47.600 --> 01:11:50.620
If they don't, then they're lost.

01:11:50.620 --> 01:11:51.940
Then I move on with something else.

01:11:51.940 --> 01:11:56.031
But I'm not going to freeze now, and say,
"Oh, my God, that if I lose…"

01:11:56.031 --> 01:11:59.810
But then, I would've never run in the first
place, if I would be afraid to run, right?

01:11:59.810 --> 01:12:03.659
You never know how far it's going to take
you.

01:12:03.659 --> 01:12:05.140
Simple rules like that.

01:12:05.140 --> 01:12:10.690
I wanted to have those people take away those
rules, or, for instance, giving back.

01:12:10.690 --> 01:12:17.440
As soon as you realize that you're not a self-made
man, and you realize that we all were created

01:12:17.440 --> 01:12:26.929
by your parents, and that you were created
by mentors, teachers, coaches, and many other

01:12:26.929 --> 01:12:34.540
people that none of us know, but you yourself
know — those are the people that have created

01:12:34.540 --> 01:12:35.540
me.

01:12:35.540 --> 01:12:40.050
Just alone, if I wouldn't have had Joe Weaver
to bring me over to America, how could I have

01:12:40.050 --> 01:12:41.340
come to America?

01:12:41.340 --> 01:12:43.530
How can I say I was self-made?

01:12:43.530 --> 01:12:48.770
How could I have become governor, if not 5.8
million people voted for me?

01:12:48.770 --> 01:12:50.929
I'm not a dictator.

01:12:50.929 --> 01:12:54.510
I was voted in the Democratic process.

01:12:54.510 --> 01:12:57.400
Did I make myself governor?

01:12:57.400 --> 01:12:58.400
No.

01:12:58.400 --> 01:12:59.400
I'm not a self-made man.

01:12:59.400 --> 01:13:05.940
I have to recognize that my training, my money,
everything comes from a lot of different people.

01:13:05.940 --> 01:13:12.550
And therefore, that means, when you recognize
that, that you now have the responsibility

01:13:12.550 --> 01:13:15.010
of going out and help others.

01:13:15.010 --> 01:13:21.430
"There are so many people out there that need
help," like my father-in-law said.

01:13:21.430 --> 01:13:30.860
Sargent Shriver, who created the Peace Corps,
Head Start, Job Corps, and all of these great

01:13:30.860 --> 01:13:40.220
programs, in his 60s, he said to a bunch of
Yale students at the graduation class, he

01:13:40.220 --> 01:13:45.219
said, "Tear down this mirror that you always
look at yourself.

01:13:45.219 --> 01:13:49.610
Tear down this mirror, and you will be able
to look beyond that mirror.

01:13:49.610 --> 01:13:54.550
And you will see the millions of people that
need your help."

01:13:54.550 --> 01:13:55.820
That's exactly right.

01:13:55.820 --> 01:14:01.760
As soon as we stop for a minute, looking at
ourselves, then you will be able to look beyond

01:14:01.760 --> 01:14:05.260
yourself, when you see that there are people
out there that need help.

01:14:05.260 --> 01:14:07.750
There are poor people out there that need
help.

01:14:07.750 --> 01:14:11.889
There are fire victims right now, out there,
that need your help.

01:14:11.889 --> 01:14:14.889
There are earthquake victims out there.

01:14:14.889 --> 01:14:16.120
There are homeless people out there.

01:14:16.120 --> 01:14:18.201
There are war veterans out there.

01:14:18.201 --> 01:14:24.380
There are kids that come from poor backgrounds
that are out there, that need to learn how

01:14:24.380 --> 01:14:29.350
to speak English, how to write English, how
to do math, and all the stuff.

01:14:29.350 --> 01:14:31.760
Immigrants that don't even speak English.

01:14:31.760 --> 01:14:37.261
There's so many areas where you can be helpful,
that takes money, or takes no money, just

01:14:37.261 --> 01:14:38.261
effort.

01:14:38.261 --> 01:14:41.890
Never, ever think it's all just about you.

01:14:41.890 --> 01:14:46.100
Someone helped you, where you are today, so
now, you go out and help someone else.

01:14:46.100 --> 01:14:52.989
This is another one of my lessons, is just
break that mirror in front of you.

01:14:52.989 --> 01:15:00.540
Those are the different lessons that I teach
people, that really had a profound impact

01:15:00.540 --> 01:15:07.840
on me, and made me successful, and the "No
Bullshit" rules.

01:15:07.840 --> 01:15:09.239
And anyone can follow it.

01:15:09.239 --> 01:15:10.239
That's what it is.

01:15:10.239 --> 01:15:14.680
Tim Ferriss: People can find the new book,
Be Useful: Seven Tools for Life, anywhere

01:15:14.680 --> 01:15:16.650
fine books are sold.

01:15:16.650 --> 01:15:19.620
They can go to beusefulbook.com.

01:15:19.620 --> 01:15:22.830
The newsletter can be found at arnoldspumpclub.com.

01:15:22.830 --> 01:15:24.970
And we'll link to all this in the show notes
at tim.blog/podcast.

01:15:24.970 --> 01:15:29.219
Arnold, is there anything else that you would
like to say?

01:15:29.219 --> 01:15:32.220
Any closing comments, or other requests of
the audience, suggestions?

01:15:32.220 --> 01:15:35.750
Anything at all, that you'd like to add before
we come to a close?

01:15:35.750 --> 01:15:36.850
Arnold Schwarzenegger: No, no.

01:15:36.850 --> 01:15:44.010
I just wanted to say to the people — I want
to thank them for having been such great supporters

01:15:44.010 --> 01:15:48.360
of mine.

01:15:48.360 --> 01:15:51.670
Without them, as I said earlier, I will be
nothing.

01:15:51.670 --> 01:15:57.130
If it wouldn't have been for the bodybuilding
fans, as I grew up, they would cheering there,

01:15:57.130 --> 01:16:01.960
and screaming, "Arnold, Arnold," motivated
me, I will be nothing.

01:16:01.960 --> 01:16:06.150
If it wouldn't have been for the movie fans,
that went to run to see Conan the Barbarian,

01:16:06.150 --> 01:16:11.450
and made it the number-one box office that
then gave me all the headlines, I would've

01:16:11.450 --> 01:16:12.580
been nothing.

01:16:12.580 --> 01:16:16.840
All the Jim Camerons, and the Ivan Reitmans,
and all of these people, and the people that

01:16:16.840 --> 01:16:21.210
are not following my newsletter, the Pump
Club, and all of these people that are even

01:16:21.210 --> 01:16:26.310
interested in a book like Be Useful.

01:16:26.310 --> 01:16:32.179
And they'd go to my seminars, and listen to
my speeches, my motivational speeches.

01:16:32.179 --> 01:16:38.370
I just really love that I have had such an
extraordinary following, and that some of

01:16:38.370 --> 01:16:45.230
my speeches have reached five and a half,
six billion people.

01:16:45.230 --> 01:16:47.460
That's really extraordinary.

01:16:47.460 --> 01:16:48.790
I want to say thank you.

01:16:48.790 --> 01:16:50.489
Without them, I will be nothing.

01:16:50.489 --> 01:16:53.070
And thank you to America, for giving me everything.

01:16:53.070 --> 01:16:56.040
Tim Ferriss: Thank you, Arnold, for the time,
yet again.

01:16:56.040 --> 01:16:57.110
Arnold Schwarzenegger: Absolutely.

01:16:57.110 --> 01:16:58.170
Thank you, Tim.

